rewards-warning-heading = What stopping this prover does — and what it does not do
rewards-warning-block-1 = This distributor pays out without you. Accrual and payouts happen on chain and need no permission from anyone — they keep running while this computer is asleep, updating, or switched off. The prover does not decide whether peers are paid. It decides which peers are paid.
rewards-warning-block-2 = What stops is the paid list. While the prover is stopped the entry set is frozen. Peers that have stopped mirroring your data keep earning from it, and peers that begin mirroring cannot be added, so they earn nothing at all. Payment to the frozen list continues the entire time.
rewards-warning-block-3 = No $DIG is lost. What you put in stays in the reserve. Funding you have committed to epochs that have not started yet can be clawed back at any time: you recover 90% of a withdrawn commitment and 10% stays in the reserve, where it is later paid out to whichever peers hold entries at that time — not to the peers whose costs it is priced against.
rewards-warning-block-4 = How big this can get, as a number. You are committing { $committed_epochs } future epochs of funding, { $committed_amount } in total. A stopped prover can therefore pay a frozen list for at most { $committed_epochs } more epochs — { $committed_days } days at this distributor's epoch length of { $epoch_days } days — and no further, because an epoch you have not funded has nothing in it to drain. Committing more epochs at once raises that number by exactly that much.
rewards-warning-block-5 = One thing here can never be undone. Adding and removing peers is authorized only by the manager key you are about to create. If that key is lost, the entry set is frozen permanently — no one, including you, can ever add or remove a peer again, while payment to whoever is on the list at that moment carries on. The only protection is to give the manager a recovery-capable key now — for example a 2-of-3 you hold two of — and that choice exists only on this screen. It is fixed for the life of the distributor the moment this spend is signed, and cannot be added later.
rewards-warning-closing = Downtime does not pause payment. It hands payment to a list that has stopped being true.
rewards-clawback-confirm-title = Withdraw your commitment for epoch { $epoch_index }?
rewards-clawback-confirm-body = You committed { $slot_amount } to epoch { $epoch_index }, which starts { $epoch_start_date }. { $returned_amount } returns to this wallet. { $forfeited_amount } stays in the distributor's reserve. That share is not returned to you and it does not go to the peers who mirrored for you — it is paid out to whichever peers hold entries when the reserve next pays, which may be nobody you have ever seen. Epoch { $epoch_index } then has no funding. Peers that pass their challenges in that epoch earn nothing from it. This withdraws only this one commitment. Your other commitments and the reserve's current epoch are untouched. This is authorized by the key recorded when you committed — { $clawback_ph_short } — and nothing else can authorize it.
rewards-clawback-withdraw-button = Withdraw { $returned_amount }
rewards-clawback-keep-button = Keep the commitment
rewards-donation-label = Donate to the current epoch (cannot be withdrawn)
rewards-donation-body = Adds $DIG to the epoch that is running now. This creates no commitment, so there is nothing to withdraw against and no clawback: once this spend confirms the $DIG is gone from your control permanently. To fund a distributor in a way you can reverse, use Commit funding above.
rewards-donation-confirm-last-line = This $DIG cannot be recovered by you or anyone else.
rewards-donation-confirm-button = Donate { $amount } permanently
rewards-donation-cancel-button = Cancel
